# Compliance Policy (mandatory)

These rules override any request for a faster or prettier shortcut that breaks them. If you cannot meet a rule, say so explicitly and stop; do not silently skip it.

## Jurisdiction
- Design for Canada first: PIPEDA and Quebec Law 25.
- Under Law 25, personal information must not leave Quebec until a privacy impact assessment and a written agreement exist. Make data region and cross-border transfer explicit, configurable per tenant, and blocked by default where required.
- Europe and GDPR come later. Do not design them out: keep data region selectable, support access/export/deletion per data subject, and keep a lawful-basis field possible.

## Audit trail
- Record an audit entry for every tool call and every handoff: timestamp, tenant, actor, action, outcome. Audit records are append-only and contain no raw secrets or unneeded personal data.

## SOC 2
- Do not claim SOC 2 compliance or a SOC 2 program in code, comments, docs, or UI copy. None exists.
- Do keep the product able to support it: role-based access control, encryption in transit (TLS everywhere, no plain HTTP), and audit logs.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
// SOC 2 compliant storage layer

// ✅ GOOD (no claim; just the control)
// Writes are tenant-scoped and recorded in the audit log.
```

## Human handoff
- A handoff is a client-configured link or phone number shown to the user.
- Do not push personal data or the conversation transcript into a third-party chat or ticketing system by default. Any such transfer must be an explicit, client-configured, documented option.
